        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1181 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Traditionally ascribed to King Gebre Meskel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a> (r. c. 1181–1221); more likely shaped in several phases, the work perhaps running into the 14th century (David Buxton), with some churches possibly first carved between 600 and 800 (David Phillipson)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">Eleven churches cut downward out of scoriaceous basalt with hammers and chisels, four free-standing monoliths and seven joined to the rock, linked by trenches and tunnels in groups north and south of the town's river Jordan, at around 2,480 m</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a>, a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lasta">Lasta</a> district of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> Region, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>; in use by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox Church</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">churches carved from the living rock; a layout representing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>; Biete Giorgis, the Church of Saint George; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a> Cross of Biete Medhane Alem; pilgrimage at Genna and Timkat; doors and windows in the Aksumite style</span></div>
