    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Tradition holds that Christ showed Gebre Meskel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and told him to build a second Jerusalem in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and the churches' layout and names are widely taken to represent the holy city.</li><li>Their dating is disputed: tradition gives them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a>'s reign, Buxton thought the work ran into the 14th century, and Phillipson argues that some began as Aksumite-era structures of 600–800.</li><li>Carved from the top down out of the rock, they follow local building traditions of Aksumite origin and remain in daily use for worship and pilgrimage.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Francisco Álvares, who saw the churches in the 1520s, swore by God that all he had written of them was true, fearing that otherwise he would not be believed.</p></aside>
    </section>

